narrative:
## Guarding Against Empty Values

Empty strings are a constant source of bugs. A name, an email, a product SKU — none should ever be empty. With an invariant type, you enforce this at the type level:

```xiom
type NonEmptyStr = {
  value: Str;
  invariant: string.str_len(value) > 0;
}
```

Now the type system itself prevents emptiness. Functions that receive `NonEmptyStr` never need to check `is_empty()` — the invariant guarantees validity.

### Safe Construction and Mutation
```xiom
fn NonEmptyStr.new(s: Str) -> Option[NonEmptyStr] {
  if string.str_len(s) == 0 { return None; }
  return Some(NonEmptyStr{ value: s });
}

fn NonEmptyStr.set(self: &mut Self, s: Str)
  requires: string.str_len(s) > 0;
{
  self.value = s;
}

fn NonEmptyStr.greet(self) -> Str {
  return "Hello, " + self.value + "!";
}
```

The constructor uses `Option` because raw strings might be empty. The setter uses `requires` because the caller must guarantee non-emptiness. Once a `NonEmptyStr` exists, you can freely call `.greet()` — it will never print "Hello, !".

